WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:03.540
You're going to learn how to say I
don't know the right way and maintain

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:05.590
your credibility in the process.

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:08.010
So here's a situation
I've seen numerous times.

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:11.120
A professional shares some important
information at a group meeting.

00:00:11.594 --> 00:00:14.204
or someone is in a public
speaking situation.

00:00:14.495 --> 00:00:18.625
All is going well so far, but once
the question and answer period

00:00:18.625 --> 00:00:21.875
starts, somebody asks a question
and the speaker doesn't know

00:00:21.875 --> 00:00:24.285
the answer and they fall apart.

00:00:24.555 --> 00:00:27.734
They look like a deer stunned
in the headlights of a car.

00:00:28.075 --> 00:00:31.439
They act like they've been caught
and they are very hesitant to

00:00:31.439 --> 00:00:32.965
say the words, I don't know.

00:00:33.295 --> 00:00:36.175
But sometimes that's
exactly what you should say.

00:00:36.395 --> 00:00:40.675
Saying I don't know is always better
than faking it and attempting to

00:00:40.725 --> 00:00:42.815
BS your way through the answer.

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:46.870
If you try to make something up or
swerve around the answer, people

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:50.810
will notice and it'll almost
always cost you some credibility.

00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:53.630
But don't worry, I'm going to show
you exactly how to say, I don't know.

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:57.159
And you're going to be so smooth
by the end of this video, nobody

00:00:57.210 --> 00:00:58.740
will even notice you didn't know.

00:00:59.069 --> 00:01:02.449
I first learned these basic tips
that we'll share when I worked for

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:04.840
a longtime mentor, Allen Weiner.

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:06.600
I've mentioned him in a lot of videos.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:10.515
He's the author of So Smart But how
intelligent people lose credibility

00:01:10.515 --> 00:01:11.725
and how they can get it back.

00:01:12.145 --> 00:01:14.414
We're going to use the
slice method in this video.

00:01:14.414 --> 00:01:18.375
The first slice is for beginners, the
second slice is an intermediate version,

00:01:18.414 --> 00:01:22.975
and the third slice is for advanced
application and it is smooth as silk.

00:01:23.415 --> 00:01:25.575
Slice 1 is the biggest slice in the video.

00:01:25.824 --> 00:01:28.445
When you don't know the answer
to a question, especially in that

00:01:28.445 --> 00:01:32.805
group situation, there are three
acceptable ways to say I don't know.

00:01:33.215 --> 00:01:37.485
One perfectly acceptable answer that will
help you maintain your credibility is to

00:01:37.485 --> 00:01:41.835
say some version of, I don't know, but
I will look into it and get back to you.

00:01:41.935 --> 00:01:44.815
Or, I don't know, but I'll
find out and follow up.

00:01:45.474 --> 00:01:48.395
I've seen this done a thousand times
from really confident speakers.

00:01:48.395 --> 00:01:49.275
That's how they do it.

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:53.400
Now, the key with all of these is that
you have to maintain your composure.

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:55.710
Don't hesitate or act embarrassed.

00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:58.969
So, if somebody asks you a question
and you don't know the answer, but

00:01:58.969 --> 00:02:03.549
perhaps you should know the answer
based upon your role, you just pause,

00:02:03.799 --> 00:02:07.189
think for a moment, and then simply
reply with some version of, I don't

00:02:07.189 --> 00:02:11.210
know, or I'm not sure, but I'll look
into it and come back to you shortly.

00:02:11.690 --> 00:02:14.830
We're still talking about Slice1,
and the next way to say, I don't

00:02:14.830 --> 00:02:19.430
know, is to then refer them to
somebody who does know the answer.

00:02:19.825 --> 00:02:23.945
There are many situations where somebody
may ask you a question that is genuinely

00:02:23.984 --> 00:02:28.525
outside of the scope of your job or
outside of the realm of information that

00:02:28.525 --> 00:02:30.335
you just delivered in your presentation.

00:02:30.735 --> 00:02:34.875
You're not expected to know everything
just because they're asking you, but

00:02:34.895 --> 00:02:38.384
if there's someone around that you
could refer them to, that's the expert

00:02:38.384 --> 00:02:39.554
who you want to point them towards.

00:02:39.584 --> 00:02:43.874
And it sounds like this, I don't know,
but Gary should have that information.

00:02:44.064 --> 00:02:48.094
Or I don't know, but I would refer you
to Gary who works in that department.

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:51.020
A couple of points of
clarification about this one.

00:02:51.310 --> 00:02:54.190
You want to be sure the person you
are mentioning really does know

00:02:54.190 --> 00:02:57.930
the answer, or at least they should
know the answer better than you.

00:02:58.169 --> 00:03:01.269
Do your best to identify the right
person for the job, in other words.

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:04.629
And if the person is in the room
at the time, that's fantastic.

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:07.689
Now, on the one hand, they can answer
the question right there and then.

00:03:07.889 --> 00:03:10.620
On the other hand, make sure
you don't throw them under the

00:03:10.630 --> 00:03:12.410
bus or blame them for anything.

00:03:12.410 --> 00:03:15.730
So refer to them in a supportive
and in a conversational way.

00:03:16.235 --> 00:03:19.305
If somebody asks you a question and
you believe that Gary might be the

00:03:19.305 --> 00:03:22.955
right person to answer it, you would
say respectfully, Is Gary here?

00:03:23.305 --> 00:03:25.145
Gary, would you like to
answer that question?

00:03:25.925 --> 00:03:29.094
And the third way to say I don't
know and maintain credibility is

00:03:29.095 --> 00:03:30.465
to tell them what you do know.

00:03:30.874 --> 00:03:34.395
I learned this tip from an old
consultant friend of mine, Joe Jokowicz.

00:03:34.404 --> 00:03:35.375
So thank you, Joe.

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:39.710
There are many occasions where you'll
be asked a question that is slightly

00:03:39.890 --> 00:03:43.650
off focus or may reflect the other
person's agenda a little too much.

00:03:43.850 --> 00:03:47.419
Their question may not be as relevant as
they're making it sound in the moment.

00:03:47.619 --> 00:03:51.060
In other instances, they may be asking
you for data or information that

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:54.764
simply doesn't exist or doesn't exist
yet or you don't have access to it.

00:03:55.234 --> 00:03:58.755
In situations like this, you would
say, I don't know, but here's what I

00:03:58.765 --> 00:04:01.515
do know, or here's what I can tell you.

00:04:02.005 --> 00:04:05.285
And then you give them the best
information that you have, that at

00:04:05.285 --> 00:04:07.565
least somewhat addresses their concern.

00:04:08.105 --> 00:04:12.145
So let's say, for example, somebody
asks you about the exact sales figures

00:04:12.155 --> 00:04:15.175
for the quarter that just closed, and
you don't know the answer to that.

00:04:15.595 --> 00:04:18.954
You might say, I don't know the exact
numbers, but I have heard it was

00:04:18.954 --> 00:04:21.125
our strongest quarter in two years.

00:04:21.714 --> 00:04:24.484
If someone asks, how many
people were there last night?

00:04:24.484 --> 00:04:27.825
You could say, I don't know how many,
but I can tell you it was packed

00:04:27.825 --> 00:04:29.655
and there was a line out the door.

00:04:30.135 --> 00:04:34.474
You notice in my examples, I'm not trying
to be argumentative or change the subject.

00:04:34.784 --> 00:04:36.585
I'm not trying to dodge the question.

00:04:36.715 --> 00:04:38.375
I just don't know the exact answer.

00:04:38.715 --> 00:04:42.385
So I try to give them something
that's valuable that corresponds to

00:04:42.385 --> 00:04:44.664
their underlying concern or question.

00:04:45.014 --> 00:04:46.275
That's when you use this option.

00:04:46.294 --> 00:04:49.984
I don't know, but here's what I do
know, or here's what I can tell you.

00:04:50.405 --> 00:04:53.825
So far, that was all Slice 1,
the biggest slice of the video.

00:04:54.005 --> 00:04:57.924
Now let's move on to Slice 2, which
is an intermediate way to answer.

00:04:58.330 --> 00:05:02.070
Use this approach if you simply want
to avoid saying the actual words

00:05:02.070 --> 00:05:03.930
quote I don't know for some reason.

00:05:04.210 --> 00:05:06.359
Maybe you don't like how it sounds
or at least you don't want to

00:05:06.359 --> 00:05:09.860
say it repetitively if people
ask you multiple questions.

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:13.739
If that's the case, there are lots of
other ways to say that that sound a

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:15.300
little better, but are still honest.

00:05:15.710 --> 00:05:17.030
Here are some other ways to say it.

00:05:17.690 --> 00:05:19.779
I haven't seen any data about that yet.

00:05:20.049 --> 00:05:23.710
Or, I can't be certain because
and then fill in the reason.

00:05:24.110 --> 00:05:28.435
Or, We'll have that answer by and
then give them a time estimate.

00:05:28.855 --> 00:05:31.315
I'd have to confirm
before I comment on that.

00:05:32.235 --> 00:05:35.415
I don't have that answer right
now, but And then you would give

00:05:35.415 --> 00:05:37.375
them whatever answer you did have.

00:05:37.905 --> 00:05:41.144
In fact, I'll bet as you're watching
this right now, you've probably heard

00:05:41.145 --> 00:05:44.825
other ways to say, I don't know,
that sound pretty good to your ears.

00:05:45.155 --> 00:05:46.355
So I have a request.

00:05:46.685 --> 00:05:51.245
Do all future viewers a favor of
this video and suggest an alternative

00:05:51.245 --> 00:05:54.535
wording that sounds good to you
without saying, I don't know,

00:05:54.695 --> 00:05:56.335
sounds supportive, it sounds honest.

00:05:56.345 --> 00:05:59.125
Put your suggestions in the
comments below for everybody to see.

00:05:59.445 --> 00:06:01.625
Now here's what to do with
these alternative phrases.

00:06:02.114 --> 00:06:06.804
If you have an upcoming presentation or
group meeting and you anticipate some

00:06:06.804 --> 00:06:10.984
Q&amp; A and you may not know the answer,
practice a few of these alternative

00:06:10.984 --> 00:06:12.574
ways to say I don't know aloud.

00:06:12.684 --> 00:06:14.374
Have them in your back pocket.

00:06:14.624 --> 00:06:18.984
Now I recommend practicing them out
loud so they sound fluent and confident

00:06:18.985 --> 00:06:20.784
coming out of your mouth in the moment.

00:06:21.230 --> 00:06:24.050
Slice 2 is a little more advanced
than Slice 1 because you don't

00:06:24.050 --> 00:06:27.340
directly say I don't know, but you're
still being honest and truthful.

00:06:27.610 --> 00:06:31.500
Using different language may help you
save face a little bit in the moment

00:06:31.700 --> 00:06:33.750
and project confidence as you answer.

00:06:33.930 --> 00:06:38.830
And you can follow up any of those phrases
again by saying, I'll follow up with you.

00:06:39.049 --> 00:06:42.270
Refer them to somebody else who does
know or tell them what you do know.

00:06:42.825 --> 00:06:44.125
And now for Slice 3.

00:06:44.295 --> 00:06:48.815
This is a little more advanced, but you
can use this advice fairly quickly with a

00:06:48.815 --> 00:06:51.385
few minutes of practice on Slice 1 and 2.

00:06:51.385 --> 00:06:54.574
And here's the tip for helping
you sound as smooth as silk.

00:06:55.005 --> 00:06:58.624
Skip over the part of the sentence
that says, I don't know, altogether.

00:06:58.855 --> 00:07:01.435
and just say the second
part of your answer.

00:07:01.655 --> 00:07:04.975
Here's some earlier examples and we're
just going to leave off the statement

00:07:05.015 --> 00:07:07.635
I don't know and say the second part.

00:07:07.835 --> 00:07:11.115
You'll see that with some minor
adjustments in your wording, this will

00:07:11.125 --> 00:07:13.344
still work great in most situations.

00:07:13.925 --> 00:07:16.495
I'll have to look into that
information and get back to you.

00:07:17.415 --> 00:07:19.395
I'll find you that answer and follow up.

00:07:20.425 --> 00:07:22.065
Gary should have that information.

00:07:22.835 --> 00:07:25.315
I would refer you to Gary
who works in that department.

00:07:26.215 --> 00:07:28.795
If Gary is here, he's the
best person to answer that.

00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:33.200
I know that it was our
strongest quarter in two years.

00:07:34.219 --> 00:07:36.930
I can tell you it was packed and
there was a line out the door.

00:07:37.690 --> 00:07:40.650
And the reason this approach works
is that you are still doing your

00:07:40.660 --> 00:07:42.860
best to help the other person.

00:07:42.919 --> 00:07:44.110
It's not a dodge.

00:07:44.209 --> 00:07:47.609
We may not know the answer, but
we're still trying to provide an

00:07:47.609 --> 00:07:50.300
answer that will give them some
helpful information or at least

00:07:50.310 --> 00:07:51.520
point them in the right direction.

00:07:51.975 --> 00:07:55.755
So, even though this entire video
is about how to say I don't know and

00:07:55.755 --> 00:08:00.145
maintain credibility, there are ways to
give an honest answer without having to

00:08:00.154 --> 00:08:02.365
always say the actual words every time.

00:08:02.815 --> 00:08:06.754
I cannot emphasize enough that even though
we're really talking about just the words

00:08:06.754 --> 00:08:11.495
you say in this video, your conversational
tone, your positive and professional

00:08:11.544 --> 00:08:17.135
attitude will go a long way to projecting
confidence and credibility regardless

00:08:17.135 --> 00:08:19.125
of which of these three slices you use.

00:08:19.450 --> 00:08:23.260
As you practice this on your own, be
sure to combine the right words with

00:08:23.270 --> 00:08:25.030
the right attitude, in other words.

00:08:25.340 --> 00:08:27.630
Until next time, thanks, God
bless, and I'll see you soon.

